course: everyday
lesson: 299
title: Parks, playgrounds, woods and hiking
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Being outdoors is wonderful, and a few simple habits keep it safe: watch wild animals from far away, share paths kindly, dress so nothing can catch, and know what to do if you get lost.
explain: Wild animals, even tame-looking squirrels in a city park, can bite, scratch or kick. Food from people is bad for them, so we watch and never feed or touch.
explain: If you lose your group in the woods, the rule is "Hug a tree": stop, stay where you are and call for help. Three blasts on a whistle is a signal that means "I need help". Searchers find someone who stays put far more easily than someone who keeps walking.
explain: Example: a boy in Colorado slips away from his family on a forest trail. He remembers the rule, sits by a big tree and blows his whistle three times every few minutes. His dad, coming back along the trail, finds him within minutes.
explain: Edges of cliffs and quarries can crumble without warning, and quarry water is very deep and very cold even on a hot day, so we keep well back.
fact: If a squirrel comes close for food, do not feed or touch it; watch it from a distance.
fact: Wild animals can bite or scratch, and food from people is bad for them.
fact: On a shared park path, ride your bike or scooter slowly, keep to one side, ring your bell and give way to walkers.
fact: Walkers, small children and dogs can step in front of a bike without warning.
fact: Take off scarves, bike helmets and anything with cords before playing on a climbing frame, because they can catch and strangle a child.
fact: Keep off playground equipment covered in ice, because ice is very slippery and falls on it are hard.
fact: If you are separated from your group in the woods, stop, stay where you are, hug a tree, and blow a whistle three times or shout.
fact: It is easier for searchers to find someone who stays in one place.
fact: If you see a deer, fox or other wild animal, stay still and quiet, watch from far away, and never feed or touch it.
fact: Tell a grown-up early about a blister and cover it with a plaster or blister pad, before it becomes an open sore.
fact: Stay well back from cliff edges and behind any fence, because edges can crumble and a fall can be deadly.
fact: Check the trail markers at every junction so you stay on the right path and can find your way back.
fact: Keep away from quarries and old mines: do not climb or swim there, because rocks fall and the water is very deep and cold.
exercise: A squirrel in the park runs right up to you as if it wants food. What should you do? | Watch it from a distance, without feeding or touching it | Give it a bit of your sandwich so it learns to trust you and stays | Pick it up gently to stroke it | Hold out your hand so it can sniff you
exercise: Why should you not feed park squirrels? | Food from people is bad for them and they can bite | Squirrels only eat at night | Feeding them is the job of the park keeper, not visitors like you | Squirrels never eat human food
exercise: You are riding your scooter on a busy park path. What is right? | Go slowly, keep to one side, ring your bell and give way to walkers | Go fast so you can pass all the people quickly and get out of their way | Ride in the middle so everyone sees you | Ring your bell and expect walkers to jump aside
exercise: Why must cyclists give way to walkers on a shared path? | Walkers, children and dogs can step out without warning | Walkers always have a map and know the right path to take | Bikes are not allowed in parks | Walkers move faster than bikes
exercise: You arrive at the climbing frame wearing a scarf and your bike helmet. What should you do? | Take them both off before you climb | Keep the helmet on to protect your head | Tuck the scarf in tightly and keep climbing | Only take them off for the big slide
exercise: Why are cords and scarves dangerous on a climbing frame? | They can catch on the equipment and strangle a child | They make your hands slippery so you slide off the bars | They scratch the paint | They make you too hot
exercise: In January the slide and swings are covered in ice. What should you do? | Keep off the icy equipment | Slide down fast, it is more fun on ice | Climb up slowly in your gloves | Use only the icy steps, not the top
exercise: You lose your class on a walk in the woods. What should you do? | Stop, stay where you are and blow your whistle three times | Run back the way you think you came until you see someone you know | Keep walking until you find a road | Hide so you stay safe until dark
exercise: Why is "hug a tree" good advice when you are lost? | Searchers find someone who stays in one place more easily | Trees keep wild animals away | You can climb the tree to see your home and walk back on your own | Trees show which way is north
exercise: You see a deer in a forest clearing. What is the right thing to do? | Stay still and quiet and watch from far away | Walk up very slowly and quietly so you can stroke it | Hold out an apple to feed it | Chase it to see how fast it runs
exercise: On a long walk your heel starts to rub and sting. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up early and cover it with a plaster or blister pad | Keep going and say nothing until the end, it will stop hurting soon | Take off your boots and walk barefoot | Pop it with a stick straight away
exercise: You reach a cliff top with a beautiful view. Where should you stand? | Well back from the edge and behind any fence | At the very edge for the best photo | Sitting with your legs over the edge | Just past the fence where the view is clear
exercise: Why should you keep back from cliff edges even when they look solid? | Edges can crumble without warning | The wind is always much stronger right at the edge | Seabirds attack people who stand near edges | You can see much less near the edge
exercise: You come to a fork in a trail with a coloured marker post. What should you do? | Check the marker to stay on the right path | Just take whichever of the paths looks shorter | Guess and keep going without looking at the post | Always turn left at every single fork you reach
exercise: On a hot day you find an old quarry full of clear water. What is right? | Stay away and do not swim or climb there | Swim only near the edge, where the water looks shallow and calm | Jump in to cool off quickly | Climb the rocks to get a better look
test: A girl in Melbourne sees a possum in a tree near her house. What should she do? | Watch it from a distance without feeding or touching it | Feed it some bread so it comes closer and you can see it better | Pick it up to cuddle it | Poke it with a stick to wake it
test: Which bike rider is behaving well on a park path? | The one who slows down near a family with a toddler | The one who rings and speeds past walkers | The one who rides on the grass, weaving between the people walking | The one who races his friend down the middle
test: Why might a hoodie with long cords be a bad choice for the playground? | The cords can catch on equipment and strangle | Hoodies make you much slower going down the slide | Cords are too bright in the sun | Hoodies fall off on the swings
test: A boy in Alaska loses his family on a forest trail. Which choice makes it most likely he is found quickly? | Staying in one place and blowing three whistle blasts | Walking downhill until he finds a river to follow out of the forest | Running to look for the car park | Climbing a tall tree to look around
test: A fox walks across your path in a park in London. What is best? | Stay still and quiet and let it go on its way | Follow it quietly to see where it lives and how many cubs it has | Throw it some of your crisps | Shout to scare it off quickly
test: What would probably happen if you ignore a small rub on your foot on a long hike? | It could become a painful open sore | It would heal faster by walking on it | It would just turn into a harmless bruise | Nothing, rubs always go away by themselves
test: Which is the safest way to enjoy a view from a cliff? | Stand well back behind the fence | Sit on the edge with a grown-up | Lean over the fence to look down | Stand on a rock at the very edge
test: Why is swimming in an old quarry so dangerous, even for good swimmers? | The water is very deep and cold, and rocks can fall | Quarry water is always salty and stings your eyes badly | Fish in quarries bite | The water is too warm to swim in
test: squirrel came right up to me!! give it my crisps? | No, watch from far away, people food is bad for it | Yes, squirrels love crisps | Yes, but hold it still so it can't bite you | Feed it just a little bit
test: lost the group in the woods, which way do i go | Stop, stay put, blow the whistle three times | Keep walking until you find a road or a house | Run back the way you think you came | Climb a tall tree to look around
test: off to the climbing frame, still got my helmet on | Take it off first, it can catch and strangle | Keep it on to protect your head | Fine, just loosen the strap a bit and climb | Only take it off on the slide
test: blister on my heel, gonna keep hiking | Tell a grown-up now and cover it | Pop it with your fingers and keep going | Ignore it till it bursts by itself | Take your shoe off and walk barefoot
explain: A boy in Colorado left the trail in the woods to follow a deer; he got lost, and it took searchers all night to find him cold and frightened.
explain: A girl in England played on the climbing frame with her scarf on; it caught on a bar as she slid, and a teacher had to free her quickly before it choked her.
fact: Cords and scarves caught on playground equipment can strangle a child in seconds.
fact: A lost person who stays in one place is found much faster than one who keeps walking.
test: lost in the woods, should i keep walking to find the road | No, stay where you are and wait to be found | Yes, walk on until you find someone to help | Yes, follow a stream | Run to keep warm
test: wanna go on the climbing frame with my scarf | Take it off first, it can catch and choke you | Tuck it in your jacket | Tie it tighter | It's fine if you go slowly and carefully
test: squirrel wants my crisps | Don't feed or touch it, it can bite | Hold one out for it | Pick it up gently | Feed it with your fingers, slowly
test: did u hear a kid wandered off on the scout hike and kept walking to find the path and "they searched for 2 days". lesson? | If lost, stay in one place and blow a whistle three times | Keep walking until you find the path | Climb a high hill to find a road and walk to it | Run so you get back before dark
test: my mate says a boy went too near the cliff for a selfie and "the whole edge fell into the sea with him". what's the true bit | Cliff edges can crumble, so stay well back behind the fence | Cliffs never crumble in summer | Selfies are only dangerous at night | Fences on cliffs are just decoration, the ground is always solid
test: kids say the girl who fed the squirrel and got bitten "deserved it lol". spread it? | No, that's mean; just don't feed or touch wild animals | Yes, she should've known | Yes, and make a joke of it in the class chat | Yes, it's a funny story
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a person separated from their group in woodland? | They shall stop, remain in one place, and signal by whistle or shout | They shall walk on until they reach a road | They shall climb a tree in order to seek the path from above | They shall run in any direction to find help
exam: Identify the principal reason for which scarves and cords are removed before using a climbing frame. | They may catch and strangle a child | They become dirty upon the equipment | They slow the child's climbing considerably | They are prohibited by park rules for appearance
exam: It is given that a quarry or disused mine lies near a walking route. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | It shall be kept away from, since rocks fall and its water is deep and cold | It may be swum in on warm days | It may be climbed with a friend | It is safe to enter provided no warning sign is displayed beside it
exam: State the reason for which trail markers are checked at every junction. | To remain on the right path and be able to find the way back | To count the number of junctions passed during the walk | To report damaged markers | To rest before continuing
